  {
    "episode_id": 43481,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/3/43481.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 25,
    "turn_text": "So, Congressman, I want to focus for a moment on a question about our Supreme Court, the ultimate arbiter of our constitutional law. professor of constitutional law, I was just to give you a very brief context. I was a prosecutor and early on in my career, and I was hired by then Attorney General in New Hampshire, David Souter, who was a stalwart Republican of the old school. went on to serve on the Supreme Court. I ran into him at the yogurt aisle at the shopping center after the Bush v. Gore decision, and he was just non-plus. He was outraged about the political bent of the Supreme Court. And now we have a Supreme Court that has been stacked by Donald Trump, and it includes Clarence Thomas. And Ginny Thomas claimed in her opening statement that she had never discussed her post-election activities with her husband. She denied that he's ever discussed his work at the court with her because it's an ironclad rule in their home. Is any of that credible? And if they're not, if those denials are not credible, What needs to happen? Are there consequences? What are the next steps? And many on the left have been calling for extreme measures about the Supreme Court. I mean, what's to happen with the credibility of the Supreme Court has been so undermined with this kind of apparent conflict of interest between the political activities and the decisions of Clarence Thomas, who refuses to recuse himself.",
